@@ -0,0 +1,126 @@
+# Harmony 600 (`harmony-600`): the `GSPM` image on architecture 14
+
+The Harmony 600 (USB `046d:c122`, architecture `14`, skin `71`; the European unit is skin `73`)
+belongs to Logitech's "Molson" family with the Harmony 650 and 700. It stores its configuration as
+the file `/cfg/usercfg`, which libconcord's MH protocol reads and writes (`remote_mh.cpp`). That file
+is a `GSPM` image, the Harmony One's format ([harmony_one/](../harmony_one/README.md)), with the
+differences below. Afterglow reads it and does not build it: the profile is `read-only`.
+
+Everything here was measured on **one** remote and one Logitech-compiled configuration (firmware 0.3,
+board 1.1.0, flash `0x15:0x1C`, 666,177 bytes, three devices and two activities), unless a line names
+another source. A rule measured once is a hypothesis about the next image.
+
+## Identity
+
+| Field | Value | How it was read |
+|---|---|---|
+| architecture (`PROTOCOL`) | 14 | libconcord `get_identity`, and the `.ezhex` header |
+| skin | 71 | the same; Afterglow's skin table names it "Harmony 600" |
+| flash | `0x15:0x1C` | the same |
+| board | `1.1.0` | the same |
+| configuration room | 3,997,696 bytes | `get_config_bytes_total` |
+
+## The image against the Harmony One's
+
+| Field | One (arch 12) | 600 (arch 14) |
+|---|---|---|
+| magic | `GSPM` | `GSPM` |
+| base address of absolute pointers | `0x040000` | `0x030000` |
+| `u32` at `0x08` | `0x1600` | `0x1400` |
+| master index at `0x0C` | 20 slots, slot 8 absent | 18 slots, all present |
+| `LWJL` button-map marker | `0x63` | `0x5B` (two fewer index slots) |
+| end vector at `0x04` | points at the final `PTYY` | the same |
+| checksum | two XOR lanes seeded `0x21`, `0x43`, over whole pairs before it | the same: computed equals stored |
+
+The base follows from the end vector: `end vector − (size − 4)` is `0x030000` on the measured image.
+
+### Slots and the One's section numbers
+
+| One section | Role in the One's reader | 600 slot |
+|---|---|---|
+| 0 to 7 | names, …, device command index (5), screens (6), fonts (7) | 0 to 7 |
+| 8 | absent on the One | absent |
+| 9 to 18 | key registers, contexts, object table, drawing programs, timers, engine, switch tables, … | 8 to 17 |
+| 19 | touch templates | absent: the 600 has no touchscreen |
+
+Measured by offering every 600 slot to every One section parser and keeping the slots each accepts
+(Harmony-remote-assistant `tools/gspm600_matrix.py`): the device index (5 → slot 5, three devices),
+fonts (7 → slot 7), the object table (11 → slot 10, 3,736 objects), drawing programs (12 → slot 11) and
+engine records (14 → slot 13) are each accepted by exactly one slot, which fixes the shift.
+
+## Commands
+
+**Command objects.** The One's is two atoms, `(command, 7D) (device, 7C)`. The 600's has a third in
+front, `(object, 7F)`, which runs another object first; the state variables name inter-device delay
+gates (`InterDeviceDelay_<id>_…`), which is the likely purpose and is not proven. On the measured image
+exactly 175 objects have the shape `7F 7D 7C`, one per command section 5 declares.
+
+**Waveforms.** Section 5 and the command records are the One's. 157 of 175 presses open with silence
+before the first mark, as every One press does; 18 open on the mark itself (a 4,500 µs leader). They are
+read with a lead-in of 0. Carrier periods measured: 26,385 ns (37.9 kHz) among others.
+
+## Screens and keys (section 6)
+
+The screen index and each screen's trailer are the One's:
+`[u16 count][00][u24 trailer]…` and `[byte][u24 header register][u16 slot count][u24 slot]…`.
+
+A **slot** differs: the One's is `[template][u24 key register][u24 program]`, seven bytes; the 600's
+is `[u24 key register][u24 program]`, six, with no template byte. The key register holds the keys a
+page answers, `[count]` then `[key code][u16 object][class]` per entry, as on the One.
+
+**Key codes** are the One's form, `0x80 | scan` for a press, with the 600's own scan codes: 54 keys,
+numbered by the keypad matrix. The table in `backends/harmony_600/keys.py` is taken from
+harmony-explorations (MIT), `reference/remotes/harmony-600/keys.md`, which measured it on a 600.
+Checked here two ways: the device-mode contexts bind exactly scans 1 to 54, and the Samsung television
+on the measured image sends, for 35 of 36 keys named, the Samsung command byte of that name. The one
+difference, PrevChannel sending `0x58` (Return) instead of `0x13` (Pre-Ch), is a mapping Logitech's
+database chose, not a key code. All Off, Enter and the two arrows below the screen were not measured
+there and are not named here; scans 6, 11, 35 and 54 remain for them.
+
+## Text
+
+Text is glyph codes as on the One. The 600's LCD fonts draw shapes Afterglow's One table does not
+hold, so the backend adds its own table, `backends/harmony_600/glyph-shapes.json`: 338 shape
+fingerprints to 75 characters, five of them drawn alike for `I` and `l` and kept as both. It was made
+from one image whose codes were read by eye from a rendered atlas and confirmed across five fonts
+(`tools/glyph_atlas.py`, `tools/make_600_glyph_shapes.py` in Harmony-remote-assistant). Shapes, not
+codes, are kept because Logitech numbers each configuration's characters its own way. With it, every
+screen of the measured image reads, the firmware's diagnostic screens among them.
+
+## Pages and their side keys
+
+A device or activity page draws the label of each side key as text, inline, `05 x y <codes> 00`, or by
+pointer to a string another page shares, `04 x y <u24>`. On the measured pages:
+
+| y | what |
+|---|---|
+| below 20 | the page title and its count ("1/5") |
+| 25 and 40 | the top labels, a line each |
+| 75 to 90 | the bottom labels |
+| 114 | the centre key below the screen ("Back", "Devices") |
+
+Labels on the left start at x 3; labels on the right are right-aligned, at x 64 or more. The corners
+are scans 8 (top left), 2 (top right), 9 (bottom left) and 34 (bottom right), with 25 the centre key.
+harmony-explorations measured 9 and 34; which of 8 and 2 is the top left was settled on the measured
+television, whose pages labelled HDMI1, HDMI2, HDMI3, SRC and 3D send exactly Samsung's codes for those.
+An import names a command after the one side key that sends it alone (`pages.rename_commands`).
+
+## Activities
+
+An activity's enter hook opens with `mode N`, and screen N draws "Starting <name>" (screens 99 and 130
+on the measured image). The One names activities from its menu, which the 600 draws with icons, so the
+600 backend takes the name from that screen when the menu gives none.
+
+## Not known
+
+- what the leading `7F` atom of a command object does, beyond running an object;
+- the scan codes of All Off, Enter and the two arrows below the screen;
+- whether skin 73 images share every rule here;
+- anything about building or writing an image: nothing has been built, and the profile is
+  `read-only`.
+
+## Sources
+
+- [harmony-explorations](https://github.com/dannybloe/harmony-explorations) (MIT): the 600's key table
+  and the architecture-14 reference, `reference/remotes/harmony-600/`.
+- [concordance / libconcord](https://github.com/jaymzh/concordance): the MH protocol, `remote_mh.cpp`.
